.TH WINSOCK_SOCKETPAIR 3 "15 Mar 2023" "CB" "Library Functions"

.SH NAME
winsock_socketpair \- Create a socket pair using Winsock

.SH SYNOPSIS
.IB #include <lib/socket.h>

.IB int
.IB wsa_socketpair(int af, int type, int proto, fd_type sock[2]);

.SH DESCRIPTION
The \fBwsa_socketpair\fR function is used to create a socket pair using the specified protocol. It uses Winsock semantics, returning SOCKET handles, not file descriptors. Currently, it supports TCP/IPv4 socket pairs only.

.SH PARAMETERS
.IB af
The address family, currently limited to AF_INET.

.IB type
The socket type, currently limited to SOCK_STREAM.

.IB proto
The protocol, currently limited to IPPROTO_IP or IPPROTO_TCP.

.IB sock
An array of two SOCKET handles, where the first element is the synchronous socket and the second element is the asynchronous socket.

.SH RETURN VALUE
The \fBwsa_socketpair\fR function returns 0 on success, or SOCKET_ERROR on failure.

.SH EXAMPLES
The following example demonstrates how to use the \fBwsa_socketpair\fR function to create a socket pair:

.nf
#include <stdio.h>
#include <lib/socket.h>

int main() {
    fd_type sock[2];
    if (wsa_socketpair(AF_INET, SOCK_STREAM, IPPROTO_IP, sock) == 0) {
        // Your code to use the socket pair here
        closesocket(sock[0]);
        closesocket(sock[1]);
    } else {
        printf("Failed to create socket pair\n");
    }
    return 0;
}
.nef